  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 326 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe much cooler and drier airmass will finally reach the Mid-\nMississippi River Valley by Wednesday, remaining in place through \nThursday. In this airmass, seasonably cool high temperatures in the \n80s F are forecast, accompanied by low dewpoints in the 50s and low-\n60s F. A surface high pressure center will also dominate the region \nwith dry weather during this time, but a series of upper-level \nshortwave troughs with northwesterly flow will induce rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms along/north of a warm front, initially in \nthe Central Plains, Thursday through Saturday. However, global \nensemble model guidance indicates that the most favored corridor of \nshowers and thunderstorms will not reach the CWA until Thursday \nnight or Friday as the warm front progresses northeastward. Although \nmoisture will be increasing through this time, the CWA will likely \nremain largely on the cool side of the front through Friday not to \nmention potential precipitation and clouds keeping temperatures \nseasonably cool.\n\nGlobal model guidance is in agreement that an upper-level ridge will \nbe amplifying across the Front Range of the Rocky Mountains through \nthe end of the week, favoring the warm front to continue lifting \nnortheastward along with the more active track of shortwave troughs. \nTherefore, confidence is high that temperatures will moderate to \naverage, if not above average through the weekend along with \nincreasingly dry conditions.",
